WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.567 align:middle line:90%


00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:05.005 align:middle line:84%
So you all agree that it is
desirable to eliminate bias,

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:05.905 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.406 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:06.906 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:09.909 align:middle line:84%
So you all think it is possible
to mitigate bias depending

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:11.411 align:middle line:90%
on what actions you take?

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:11.911 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:12.846 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:16.916 align:middle line:84%
So you do think that can make
choices and not just randomly.

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.852 align:middle line:84%
More well-grounded
choices are better than

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:22.622 align:middle line:84%
less well-grounded ones,
say, for addressing bias.

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:25.792 align:middle line:84%
I don't think we really
have a choice about things.

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.862 align:middle line:84%
People might think that they
do, but they don't really.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:32.631 align:middle line:84%
We can't choose where we are
born or how smart we are,

00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:35.769 align:middle line:84%
and we can't choose if
we get hit by a car.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:40.306 align:middle line:84%
We think we are making a
choice, but we really aren't.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:41.574 align:middle line:90%
It's like Sophie said.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.243 align:middle line:90%
It's all just an illusion.

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:43.977 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:45.045 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you're right.

00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:46.846 align:middle line:90%
Randomness doesn't help.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.415 align:middle line:90%
You've convinced me.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:48.915 align:middle line:90%
Wait.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:51.885 align:middle line:84%
Did I have a choice, or
was I compelled to agree?

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:52.385 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.921 align:middle line:90%
Your neurons told you to agree.

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:56.356 align:middle line:90%
Yes, exactly.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:01.294 align:middle line:84%
And randomness, a consequence
of a chaotic quantum process,

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:03.596 align:middle line:84%
just makes what we
do not predictable

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:06.433 align:middle line:84%
but doesn't mean that
we choose what we do.

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:07.467 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:11.104 align:middle line:84%
Well, you have a choice whether
you're going to be biased

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:12.038 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:12.639 align:middle line:90%
Mhm.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:13.840 align:middle line:90%
Indeed.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.376 align:middle line:84%
It does seem that
judgments involve choice.

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:19.079 align:middle line:84%
But Alyssa, if you were
given the full understanding

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.881 align:middle line:84%
of a situation, wouldn't you
always take the best action?

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.316 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, of course.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.050 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.320 align:middle line:84%
You can't really choose whether
anything is better or worse.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.322 align:middle line:90%
It's all just a value judgment.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.724 align:middle line:84%
You may be compelled to
do something by others,

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:34.860 align:middle line:84%
but you never really
freely choose.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.463 align:middle line:84%
Do you really believe
that, or are you

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:38.798 align:middle line:90%
just playing devil's advocate?

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:39.299 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:40.500 align:middle line:90%
A little of both.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.203 align:middle line:84%
But even if I am just
playing devil's advocate,

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.771 align:middle line:84%
what's wrong with
what I'm saying?

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:49.075 align:middle line:84%
What's wrong is that your model
of human life is inadequate.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:51.411 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't describe
what it really is.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.047 align:middle line:84%
We're not just some balls
moving around deterministically

00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:57.550 align:middle line:84%
with random noise interspersed
like a term in a Langevin

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.119 align:middle line:90%
equation.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.387 align:middle line:84%
Isn't that true,
Professor Muller?

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.356 align:middle line:90%
What a great discussion this is.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:05.091 align:middle line:84%
Sounds like you had
a much better spring

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.994 align:middle line:84%
break discussing these questions
than going to the beach.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:09.195 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLES]

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.164 align:middle line:84%
I think you're on the
right track, Sophie.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:11.664 align:middle line:90%
Funny.

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:14.834 align:middle line:84%
Coincidentally, that's
my first name too.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.537 align:middle line:84%
At any rate, you have
unveiled a great tension

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:19.372 align:middle line:90%
in modern science.

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:22.308 align:middle line:84%
We are brought up to think
that modern science is the way

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.677 align:middle line:84%
to describe the
world, but it seems

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:27.013 align:middle line:84%
inadequate to
describe human things.

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.083 align:middle line:84%
Ethical questions cannot be
reducible to mathematics--

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:34.487 align:middle line:84%
similar to justice and
beauty, hope and suffering,

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:37.757 align:middle line:84%
and, for that
matter, even truth.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.091 align:middle line:90%
Yes, Glenda.

00:02:39.091 --> 00:02:42.662 align:middle line:84%
Aren't those things even more
important than modern science?

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.097 align:middle line:90%
I would say so.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:44.831 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.867 align:middle line:84%
Those things are
arbitrary or a matter

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:51.271 align:middle line:84%
of individual's interests--
what's in it for them.

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:53.706 align:middle line:84%
That's the way to look
at them scientifically.

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:57.277 align:middle line:84%
Bias exists because people
have conflicting interests.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:59.579 align:middle line:84%
Sure, if you look at it
purely scientifically,

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:02.382 align:middle line:84%
but interest alone cannot
be adequate since people are

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:05.518 align:middle line:84%
willing to sacrifice interests
for passions and, more

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.488 align:middle line:84%
importantly, for things
beyond themselves.

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:11.024 align:middle line:84%
At any rate, do most
of us really know

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.592 align:middle line:90%
all of our interests?

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:16.162 align:middle line:84%
We all have preferences that
go far beyond interests.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:20.200 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps we can say at best that
those interests are intertwined

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.502 align:middle line:84%
with different
approaches to the world.

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:25.872 align:middle line:84%
Besides, aren't the most
boring people the ones who say,

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:29.442 align:middle line:84%
I'm right, and, even if one
can never make value judgments,

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.711 align:middle line:84%
I have my own values
and I stick with them?

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:35.415 align:middle line:84%
[CHUCKLES] Yeah, these are
the most irritating and boring

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:36.716 align:middle line:90%
people.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:40.820 align:middle line:84%
So, now let's dig down a bit
more into the mathematics

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.189 align:middle line:90%
of AI and the sorting problem.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:46.692 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps it's best to start
with a specific example.

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:50.063 align:middle line:84%
What's the reason for the
bias in facial recognition

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.831 align:middle line:84%
that we discussed
a little while ago?

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:55.001 align:middle line:84%
A biased training set due
to historical inequity.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:55.768 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.071 align:middle line:84%
So one of the issues
is the training set.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:00.006 align:middle line:84%
The other issue
is the algorithm,

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.342 align:middle line:84%
including its hyperparameters,
all leading to how

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:04.210 align:middle line:90%
the classification is done.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.879 align:middle line:84%
This is why different algorithms
give different error rates

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.848 align:middle line:90%
using the same training data.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:09.816 align:middle line:90%
Anything else?

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:13.052 align:middle line:90%


00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:13.753 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:16.456 align:middle line:90%
So those are the two key issues.

00:04:16.456 --> 00:04:18.791 align:middle line:84%
However, within the
choice of algorithm,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.327 align:middle line:84%
there is the problem
with classification.

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:23.463 align:middle line:84%
Let me project
this data set, here

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.231 align:middle line:90%
with points in red and blue.

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:28.835 align:middle line:84%
You have many choices for
putting the dividing surface.

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:31.304 align:middle line:84%
But what if we
choose the wrong one?

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:34.307 align:middle line:84%
Some of the data may be
misclassified, won't it?

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:35.308 align:middle line:90%
You mean like this?

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:36.976 align:middle line:90%
Yes, exactly.

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:41.180 align:middle line:84%
So how do you choose the
right line ahead of time?

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.216 align:middle line:90%
That is a problem.

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:47.120 align:middle line:84%
It could be even worse if
your data set looks like this.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.756 align:middle line:84%
But then we don't have
to stick with lines.

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:51.424 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you draw
a curve around them?

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:52.158 align:middle line:90%
Like this?

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:53.993 align:middle line:90%
Yes, exactly.

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.995 align:middle line:84%
Now you've classified
them all perfectly.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:56.963 align:middle line:90%
Well, wait a second.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.898 align:middle line:84%
If you do that, it works
for the training set,

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.367 align:middle line:84%
but it may not work for
any additional data sets.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.803 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it might be
even worse than the lines

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:06.139 align:middle line:84%
because you're overfitting
to the training data.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.874 align:middle line:90%
Do you mean like this?

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:08.841 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.076 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't work.

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:11.644 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yes.

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:12.545 align:middle line:90%
I see it.

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:13.579 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.049 align:middle line:84%
Remember this is a sorting
algorithm, here in two

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:19.719 align:middle line:84%
dimensions with two descriptors,
thus it is quite limited.

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:22.355 align:middle line:84%
But even if you do it in a very
large number of dimensions with

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:24.257 align:middle line:84%
a very large number
of descriptors,

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:26.592 align:middle line:84%
you are still projecting
a complicated system--

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.695 align:middle line:90%
shall we say a natural system--

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:31.397 align:middle line:84%
onto an artificial
mathematical construct

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:34.834 align:middle line:84%
that necessarily leaves
much out of the description.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:38.204 align:middle line:84%
Or if you want to stay in
the realm of mathematics,

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.940 align:middle line:84%
you could say that due to
a necessary incomplete set

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.777 align:middle line:84%
of descriptors you
were fitting noise.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:48.014 align:middle line:84%
This allows you to get a perfect
fit for your training set,

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.383 align:middle line:84%
but then you have an even
worse fit for your data set

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:52.585 align:middle line:90%
moving forward.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.187 align:middle line:90%
Patch.

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:56.689 align:middle line:84%
In other words, we
have to accept the fact

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:58.591 align:middle line:90%
that there will be errors.

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:01.761 align:middle line:84%
But at least we could distribute
the errors evenly among groups.

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:04.130 align:middle line:90%
That is what Alyssa said before.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.733 align:middle line:84%
We can try, but
then I'm worried we

00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:08.901 align:middle line:90%
won't include all the groups.

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:12.271 align:middle line:84%
Even if we could, there'll
be errors in the errors.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.374 align:middle line:84%
Unless we have a very,
very large training

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:17.310 align:middle line:84%
set, which practically
we never have,

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.911 align:middle line:90%
we won't be able to do that.

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:19.746 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:21.581 align:middle line:84%
The training set would
have to incorporate

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:23.149 align:middle line:90%
all the data in the world.

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:25.184 align:middle line:84%
You know, that
reminds me of a story

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.820 align:middle line:84%
I once heard, I think from
a South American writer,

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.623 align:middle line:84%
where someone makes a one-to-one
scale map of their country

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.525 align:middle line:90%
with all the details included.

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:33.359 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLES]

00:06:33.359 --> 00:06:35.762 align:middle line:90%
Ha, ha.

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:37.363 align:middle line:90%
Well, I grant the problem.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:40.332 align:middle line:90%
But then what do we do?

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:43.369 align:middle line:84%
We all agree that we
need to eliminate bias,

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:47.607 align:middle line:90%
but it seems so hard.

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:51.844 align:middle line:84%
What if we could at least
have a very extensive model

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:57.049 align:middle line:84%
that for all practical purposes
captures a given situation?

00:06:57.049 --> 00:07:00.486 align:middle line:84%
Which I guess is
actually impossible

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:03.489 align:middle line:84%
because it would require a
complete and accurate model

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:05.892 align:middle line:84%
of all the objects
in the whole world.

00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:06.592 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.362 align:middle line:84%
As engineers, we can at
least work to do that.

00:07:09.362 --> 00:07:13.533 align:middle line:84%
If the model is extensive
enough, we can eliminate bias.

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:17.270 align:middle line:84%
But then we'd be eliminating any
choice that we might have had.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.440 align:middle line:84%
We would be controlled
by an algorithm.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:26.012 align:middle line:84%
That sounds-- that
sounds tyrannical.

00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:26.779 align:middle line:90%
Indeed.

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:30.016 align:middle line:84%
Mathematization of human
things distorts them so much

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:32.485 align:middle line:84%
that they become something
which they are not.

00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:35.288 align:middle line:84%
This is actually the
utilitarian approach.

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:40.493 align:middle line:84%
Human things like joy, anger,
justice, honor, beauty,

00:07:40.493 --> 00:07:43.963 align:middle line:84%
and happiness are really
not mathematizable.

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:45.932 align:middle line:84%
We lose all of what
is important in them

00:07:45.932 --> 00:07:47.967 align:middle line:90%
when we mathematize them.

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:52.171 align:middle line:84%
We could in principle try to
do so and therein transform

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:52.939 align:middle line:90%
human reality.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:56.042 align:middle line:90%


00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:57.176 align:middle line:90%
Glenda.

00:07:57.176 --> 00:07:59.479 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't sound appealing.

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:01.781 align:middle line:90%
It sounds dreadful.

00:08:01.781 --> 00:08:03.883 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that wouldn't be good.

00:08:03.883 --> 00:08:07.353 align:middle line:84%
But we can at least do
it partially, can't we?

00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:08.054 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:09.856 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that would
be a good solution.

00:08:09.856 --> 00:08:10.756 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:12.925 align:middle line:84%
Even if those human
things cannot be described

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:16.395 align:middle line:84%
by mathematics, it is still the
only rigorous way to understand

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:17.129 align:middle line:90%
the world.

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:22.101 align:middle line:84%
I'm not so sure anymore, but
the bias is in the data set,

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:24.103 align:middle line:90%
and it is in the algorithms.

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:28.841 align:middle line:84%
So we must be able to adjust
our models to solve the problem.

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:30.176 align:middle line:90%
Mustn't we?

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:33.513 align:middle line:84%
Well, consider this-- our
recognition of the problem

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.648 align:middle line:90%
of bias is not mathematical.

00:08:35.648 --> 00:08:39.184 align:middle line:84%
Mathematics comes only
after such recognition.

00:08:39.184 --> 00:08:41.587 align:middle line:84%
Mathematics is something
we impose on our broader

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.356 align:middle line:90%
understanding of the world.

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:46.092 align:middle line:84%
We can use it to develop
models, but they will always

00:08:46.092 --> 00:08:48.928 align:middle line:84%
be just that, models
which are necessarily

00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:51.063 align:middle line:90%
incomplete descriptions.

00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:57.203 align:middle line:84%
I see what you're
saying, I think.

00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:58.204 align:middle line:90%
Sophie.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:01.107 align:middle line:90%
But AI is based on mathematics.

00:09:01.107 --> 00:09:04.410 align:middle line:84%
So we have to fix this
problem in the algorithms

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:07.046 align:middle line:90%
via fixing the algorithms, i.e.

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:08.481 align:middle line:90%
by using mathematics.

00:09:08.481 --> 00:09:09.682 align:middle line:90%
Don't we?

00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:11.951 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can think
of things this way,

00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:14.754 align:middle line:84%
but that will lead us
to a double whammy.

00:09:14.754 --> 00:09:16.956 align:middle line:84%
We cannot develop a
non-arbitrary definition

00:09:16.956 --> 00:09:20.593 align:middle line:84%
of the criteria for the
training set, and, two,

00:09:20.593 --> 00:09:23.095 align:middle line:84%
we cannot develop a
non-arbitrary definition

00:09:23.095 --> 00:09:27.633 align:middle line:84%
of the error target for
the sorting algorithm.

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:30.136 align:middle line:84%
Well, I knew that we
were at an impasse,

00:09:30.136 --> 00:09:32.872 align:middle line:84%
but I didn't think it
was this big of one.

00:09:32.872 --> 00:09:34.340 align:middle line:84%
Professor, your
class has made us

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:36.709 align:middle line:90%
more stuck than we were before.

00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:39.445 align:middle line:90%
So, what do we do?

00:09:39.445 --> 00:09:41.547 align:middle line:90%
Is there another way?

00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:43.182 align:middle line:90%
Indeed, Glenda, there is that.

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:47.086 align:middle line:84%
It requires a reconsideration of
our concerns and of the issue.